# Multiple-shape story exports

This implements the selected-output-variants and per-output retry parts of
migration §5B for an open story. It uses the existing shared scene renderer and
scheduler. Grouped collection execution and persistent queued story exports
remain separate requirements in the [execution ledger](MIGRATION_STATUS.md).

## User workflow

Export offers Portrait, Tall, or Both shapes, independently of the shape currently
being edited. Before preparation it shows the full file count and dimensions.
Both shapes produce a complete portrait sequence followed by a complete tall
sequence, with shape names in the filenames. A single shape retains the existing
filenames. Each output uses that shape's saved framing, crops, captions, masks,
effects and copied style definitions.

Preparation captures one immutable project revision, format and set of shapes.
Successes become individual download links as they finish, in manifest order.
An individual failure leaves successful files intact. Retry failed runs only
failed outputs; Pause cancels unfinished work and keeps ready files, and Continue
preparing runs only unfinished outputs. The attention filter shows errors and
render/layout warnings by slide and shape. Sharing uses a fresh user click and
labels a partial set explicitly. Individual downloads remain available when
sharing is unsupported.

Changing export choices releases earlier files. Prepare files then captures a
fresh plan with the new choices.
Closing the sheet or using browser Back cancels work and releases prepared file
URLs and references. Already downloaded files are unaffected. Choices persist
with the story, but prepared files and queue progress do not survive closure or
reload. The sheet asks users to download or share before closing. This is not
the durable large-folder job workflow.

## Reusable output styles

Save my style → Output captures the selected export shapes and format, separately
from the preview shape. These new definitions declare `export-variants-v1` and
retain the usual bounded, immutable, parameter-only style format. Missing
capability declarations and malformed/duplicate/unavailable shapes are rejected.
Style files do not acquire source photos, masks or captions.

Existing output styles without a shapes array keep their single-shape meaning.
Applying one clears a later multiple-shape choice rather than silently exporting
extra files. Applying a new two-shape output style restores both shapes without
changing photo corrections. Saved older stories without an explicit export
selection continue to use their preview shape.

## Concurrency and lifetime

The export plan copies and freezes metadata before dispatch. Its work items
identify a slide and variant; the shared scheduler still owns asset reads,
memory/CPU admission, priority and actual worker concurrency. Output files enter
the workspace retained-byte ledger immediately on completion. Pause does not
remove them. Changing choices and closing release their URLs and references;
late renderer completions cannot recreate them.

An outstanding Share operation has its own reservation for the total encoded
File bytes. It remains charged if the export panel closes, and is released when
the share promise settles. While the panel is open this is conservative extra
headroom in addition to its file ledger; it does not measure native/browser copy
counts or process RSS. The browser regression holds a Share promise open, closes
the panel, verifies the retained reservation, and then verifies its release.

The document model's 40-slide bound and two supported variants imply an 80-output
structural ceiling. The current 6–12-photo creation flow produces 4–8 slides, or
at most 16 outputs when both shapes are selected. Neither number is a certified
physical-phone capacity. Files are retained for sharing, so memory depends on
encoded output size as well as render working sets. Direct streaming to a sink,
persistent queues, complete retained-memory qualification and physical-device
capacity remain open.

## Verification

Eight model/coordinator tests cover ordered count/naming, copied immutable inputs,
legacy defaults, invalid choices, output-style roundtrip/compatibility, partial
failure, retry isolation, pause/continue, synchronous failures and late completion
after disposal. The full deterministic suite passes 175 checks: 38 scheduler and
137 model/diagnostic checks.

The focused Chromium test uses the actual Pillow engine, workers, saved-project
storage and phone UI. Eight outputs at each of 1/4/8 workers match live serial
PNG references, with observed peaks 1/4/8 and no admission violation. These
concurrency cases use 432×540 and 432×768 output variants. The phone workflow then
checks eight 1080×1350/1080×1920 outputs by exact hash and independent native
decoding. An injected admission failure preserves seven completed output URLs;
retry produces only the missing file. Pause retains one ready output and resumes
the other seven. Actual Share/Download actions, ordered naming, current user
activation, 200% text, saved output-style application, reload and Back during
blocked preparation are covered. Source nodes and per-slide corrections remain
unchanged.

Normal and 200% phone screenshots were visually inspected. This is browser
viewport evidence, not physical picker/keyboard/share-target qualification.
The complete source and optimized 214-file packaged Chromium suites pass,
alongside folder recovery, 33/33 adapter parity and the declared 27/35 function
coverage slice. The [retained record](research/2026-09-20/story-exports/README.md)
binds 121 processing inputs, 234 source/test/build files and 214 packaged files.
Its source hash differs from earlier timing evidence; the dirty release aggregate
remains unproven. No new throughput or production-readiness claim follows from
these checks.
